fix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,4340,1657,6785,1378,48342,25780,525,9363,315,2176,220,16,15,15,323,220,16,20,15,1939,37314,9960,510,27,37420,91339,397,1249,8253,1246,1657,6785,1378,48342,25780,525,9363,315,2176,220,16,15,15,323,220,16,20,15,11,582,1191,553,9271,279,12196,4185,49109,320,38,6484,8,315,220,16,15,15,323,220,16,20,15,382,5338,11,582,1477,279,10250,8168,8040,315,220,16,15,15,323,220,16,20,15,510,59,9640,16,15,15,284,220,17,61,17,1124,15136,220,20,61,17,198,59,921,59,9640,16,20,15,284,220,17,1124,15136,220,18,1124,15136,220,20,61,17,198,59,2533,785,479,6484,374,279,1985,315,279,15457,2355,315,1817,10250,8168,4185,311,2176,5109,510,59,9640,59,1318,90,38,6484,25547,16,15,15,11,220,16,20,15,8,284,220,17,61,16,1124,15136,220,20,61,17,284,220,20,15,198,59,21675,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,2610,1231,312,18883,55856,279,3491,323,18130,279,6291,504,279,7167,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nHow many positive two-digit integers are factors of both 100 and 150?\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo determine how many positive two-digit integers are factors of both 100 and 150, we start by finding the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 100 and 150.\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorizations of 100 and 150:\n\\[\n100 = 2^2 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\\[\n150 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor common to both numbers:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(100, 150) = 2^1 \\times 5^2 = 50\n\\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"2a3bd8d0c6cec2ca48d1c2386dcc3b33dcb66fc3b6d06240f2b5764e1d57d851"},"seconds":21.427679300308228,"seed":912305161607944673,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine how many positive two-digit integers are factors of both 100 and 150, we follow these steps:\n\n1. **Find the Prime Factorizations:**\n   \\[\n   100 = 2^2 \\times 5^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   150 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 5^2\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the Greatest Common Divisor (GCD):**\n   The GCD is found by taking the lowest power of all prime factors common to both numbers.\n   \\[\n   \\text{GCD}(100, 150) = 2^1 \\times 5^2 = 50\n   \\]\n\n3. **Identify Two-Digit Factors of the GCD:**\n   We need to find all positive two-digit factors of 50. First, list all the factors of 50:\n   \\[\n   50 = 1, 2, 5, 10, 25, 50\n   \\]\n   Among these, the only two-digit factors are:\n   \\[\n   10, 25, 50\n   \\]\n\n4. **Count the Two-Digit Factors:**\n   From the list of factors, the two-digit factors of 50 are 10, 25, and 50. Therefore, there are 3 such factors.\n\nThus, the number of positive two-digit integers that are factors of both 100 and 150 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{3}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To determine how many positive two-digit integers are factors of both 100 and 150, we follow these steps:\n\n1. **Find the Prime Factorizations:**\n   \\[\n   100 = 2^2 \\times 5^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   150 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 5^2\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the Greatest Common Divisor (GCD):**\n   The GCD is found by taking the lowest power of all prime factors common to both numbers.\n   \\[\n   \\text{GCD}(100, 150) = 2^1 \\times 5^2 = 50\n   \\]\n\n3. **Identify Two-Digit Factors of the GCD:**\n   We need to find all positive two-digit factors of 50. First, list all the factors of 50:\n   \\[\n   50 = 1, 2, 5, 10, 25, 50\n   \\]\n   Among these, the only two-digit factors are:\n   \\[\n   10, 25, 50\n   \\]\n\n4. **Count the Two-Digit Factors:**\n   From the list of factors, the two-digit factors of 50 are 10, 25, and 50. Therefore, there are 3 such factors.\n\nThus, the number of positive two-digit integers that are factors of both 100 and 150 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]","id":"prealgebra:567"}
